Let's define the set $$$A_i$$$ for each $$$1 \leq i \leq n$$$ as a set of columns $$$j$$$, such that the color of the cell $$$(i, j)$$$ is blue. If there exists two rows $$$1 \leq x_1 \lt x_2 \leq n$$$, such that $$$A_{x_1} 
ot\subset A_{x_2}$$$ and $$$A_{x_2} 
ot\subset A_{x_1}$$$ the good mood certificate exists. It's easy to see, because if $$$A_{x_1} 
ot\subset A_{x_2}$$$ there exists some $$$y_1$$$, such that $$$y_1 \in A_{x_1}$$$ and $$$y_1 
ot\in A_{x_2}$$$ and if $$$A_{x_2} 
ot\subset A_{x_1}$$$ there exists some $$$y_2$$$, such that $$$y_2 \in A_{x_2}$$$ and $$$y_2 
ot\in A_{x_1}$$$. Four cells $$$(x_1, y_1)$$$, $$$(x_1, y_2)$$$, $$$(x_2, y_1)$$$, $$$(x_2, y_2)$$$ will be the good mood certificate. Otherwise, if for any two rows $$$1 \leq x_1 \lt x_2 \leq n$$$ $$$A_{x_1} \subset A_{x_2}$$$ or $$$A_{x_2} \subset A_{x_1}$$$, there is no good mood certificate.
